    "question": {
      ":question/id": "32a4fc8f-a0c9-5682-9fef-d6cbe0138e91",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\sech^{2} (\\frac{4x}{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\frac{4x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\sech^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sech^2(\\frac{4x}{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\int\\sech^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\tanh(\\frac{4x}{5})+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
